.he "wsld (1)                     8/11/26                     wsld (1)"
.fo "				    -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
PROGRAM
.in +5
.br
.bd
wsld
- link editor


.in -5
.bd
USAGE
.in +5
.br
.bd
wsld [options] file ...


.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Wsld
combines object files and libraries into one module, resolving the
references between them.  The objects are in the Whitesmiths' format
that
.bd
asz
writes and
.bd
wslib
archives.  The result is an executable image unless -r says otherwise,
and is left in "a.out" if -o does not name it.

Files are taken in the order given, which matters.  An object is
linked unconditionally; a library is searched, and only those members
that resolve a reference outstanding at the time the library is
reached are taken from it.  A library named before the object that
needs it contributes nothing.  This is why the C library appears twice
in some link lines: the system-call library calls back into it, and
the linker makes one pass.

The first file is normally the startup object - crt0.o for Micronix,
crtcpm.o for CP/M - which is what sets up the arguments and calls
main.

The options are:

.in +10
.ta 10
.ti -10
-o file	Write the output to
.bd
file.
The default is "a.out".

.ti -10
-r	Emit relocatable output, to be linked again later.  The
relocation information and the symbol table are kept, and undefined
symbols are not an error.

.ti -10
-s	Strip the symbol table from the output.

.ti -10
-9	Write nine-character symbols rather than fifteen, for output
that has to be read by older tools.

.ti -10
-l x	Search the library "libx.a" at this point in the file list.

.ti -10
-L dir	Add
.bd
dir
to the list of directories searched for libraries.

.ti -10
-Ttext=n	Set the base address of the text segment.  Micronix
programs start at 0x100.

.ti -10
-Tdata=n	Set the base address of the data segment.  The default
is the end of text.

.ti -10
-Tbss=n	Set the base address of the bss segment.  The default
is the end of data.

.ti -10
-V	List the object files that were linked, with the address each
segment was given.

.ti -10
-v	Report what the link is doing.  Repeat it for more.

.in -10
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ccc (1), asz (1), wslib (1), wsnm (1), wssize (1)


.in -5
.bd
DIAGNOSTICS
.in +5
.br
A symbol that nothing defines is reported by name and the link fails,
unless -r was given.  A symbol defined twice is reported with both
definitions.
